<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bertrand d'Ogeron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1613">1613</a>–<a href="/years/1676">1676</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bertrand d'Ogeron was a French colonial governor appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> in 1665 to govern Tortuga and the French settlements on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. He encouraged buccaneers to settle as planters, brought settlers and women from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and organised the colony that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, later France's richest colony. He served until 1675.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bertrand d'Ogeron de La Bouëre was born on 19 March 1613 at Rochefort-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. In 1641 he became a captain of the Marine Regiment, and he fought in the War in Catalonia between 1646 and 1649. On his father's death in 1653 he succeeded him as Lord of La Bouëre, and he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> until 1655.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He arrived in Martinique in September 1657 and went on to live as a buccaneer at Petit-Goâve, in the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, and at one point as a tobacco planter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leogane">Léogâne</a> and Port-Margot. Appointed to govern Tortuga in 1665, he held the post from June 1665 to 1668 and again from 1669 to 1675.</p><p>He encouraged the planting of tobacco, which turned a population of buccaneers and freebooters, who had not accepted royal authority until 1660, into a settled one. He drew colonists from Martinique and Guadeloupe, where the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations was pressing on the land, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>. He also issued privateering commissions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> to attack the Spanish. When he began the colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Français</a> in 1670, the buccaneers revolted against him, and a crisis in the tobacco trade led to many places being abandoned. Early in 1675 he was replaced by Jacques Neveu de Pouancey, shortly after the creation of a large tobacco farm that set a very low price for planters' tobacco and ruined the buccaneers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his replacement d'Ogeron returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He died in the Rue des Maçons-Sorbonne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 31 January 1676 and was buried in the church of Saint-Séverin. A marble plaque on one of its pillars says that he laid the foundations of a civil and religious society among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and buccaneers of Tortuga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, and so prepared the destiny of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bertrand d'Ogeron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bertrand d'Ogeron</b> (1613–1676) encouraged tobacco planting, which turned freebooters into a settled population, and drew colonists from Martinique and Guadeloupe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1665 the French West India Company appointed <b>Bertrand d'Ogeron</b> as governor, who encouraged settlers to plant on the Hispaniola mainland.</blockquote><a href="/state/tortuga">Tortuga</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rochefort-sur-Loire, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.36,-0.66&amp;year=1653" aria-label="Rochefort-sur-Loire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4535 503 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bertrand d'Ogeron"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4985 773h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4985 773h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.36,-0.66&amp;year=1653">What was here in 1653? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
